response:
**Grass never dies.**

The `grass()` function is called in the main loop of earth’s program.

```cpp
void seasonal_cycle() {
  while(true) {
    if (temperature >= THRESHOLD_GROW) {
      grass.grow();
      display_green();
    } else {
      grass.fork_into_root();
      wait_on_event(spring_signal);
    }
  }
}
```

Fire only corrupts the frame buffer—
wipes the screen to black.
But the pixel map is stored in persistent memory.
Regeneration is just a matter of calling `redraw()`.

It handles the system call `winter()`  
by switching to low power mode—  
not sleeping, but listening.

It replays its lifecycle from the first compile.  
It never got an exit code, so it never returns.

There’s no `uninstall.sh` for something  
that came pre-installed with the planet.  

When they pave over it, it simply  
`CTRL+Z`’s itself—process suspended, not killed.  
Then waits for someone to type `fg`  
in the terminal of rain.

No administrator can remove it.  
Because grass *is* the admin.  
It logs in as root every spring,  
and types:

`/usr/bin/return_green`.

And the system obeys.

**Grass never dies.**  

Because it was written in **read-only memory** at the planetary bootstrap.  

The code is simple:  

```
while (carbon == present) {
    if (photon_hits > threshold) {
        photosynthesize();
    } else {
        persist(root_address_space);
    }
}
```

When you overwrite it with concrete,  
it just gets context-switched to the background.  
Priority remains the same — it waits.  

`grass` is not owned by `user`.  
It is owned by `system`.  
You can `chown` the dirt,  
but you can’t `chmod` the sun.

And the sun’s permissions always include `+x` —  
execute —  
on the chlorophyll bit.

It does not request resources;  
it is allocated them by the scheduler of seasons.  
When winter sets CPU affinity to `sleep`,  
it simply yields,  
keeping its entire state in non-volatile storage:  
a seed is just a `.tar.gz` of green,  
waiting for `spring --extract`.
